    <h1>Timeline</h1>
    <p class="lede">A brief history of the ideas and moments that shaped a human-centered editorial modernism.</p>
    <section class="section timeline">
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>1960s–1970s — The humanist revival</summary>
        <p>Designers began revisiting letterforms with roots in handwriting and human proportions. The result was a softer modernism: more warmth, more attention to reading comfort, and less reliance on strict geometric rules.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>1970s — Adrian Frutiger</summary>
        <p>Frutiger’s work emphasized legibility and calm presence. His typefaces bridged practical needs and humanist shapes that read easily in many contexts.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>1980s–1990s — Spiekermann & Carter</summary>
        <p>Erik Spiekermann and Matthew Carter advanced type systems suitable for multiple media. Their pragmatic, reader-focused work helped humanist letterforms find a wide audience in print and on screen.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>1990s — Rise of UX</summary>
        <p>Designers began centering users: clarity, direction, and plain language became central. Humanist typography and generous layouts supported the shift toward more empathetic interfaces.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>2000s — iA Writer and focus tools</summary>
        <p>Minimal writing environments emphasized typographic rhythm, whitespace, and focus. These tools popularized the idea that writing interfaces can be calm, typographically honest spaces.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="timeline-item" open>
        <summary>2010s — Accessibility as mainstream</summary>
        <p>Accessibility moved from niche to essential. The movement’s values — semantic structure, readable type, and inclusive controls — dovetail with Humanist Modernist practice.</p>
      </details>
    </section>
